    "lens": {
      "mechanics": "Inflation can rise when spending financed by money and credit outpaces output. Gold pays no interest, so higher bond returns after inflation increase the cost of holding it instead of an income-producing asset.",
      "data": "Three forces matter. <a href=\"https://home.treasury.gov/resource-center/data-chart-center/interest-rates/TextView?type=daily_treasury_real_yield_curve&amp;field_tdr_date_value_month=202611\">The 23 Nov real yield rose from 2.77% to 2.79%</a>, increasing that income disadvantage. <a href=\"https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/T10YIE\">Market inflation compensation fell to 2.13% from 2.15%</a>, while nominal yields held steady. <a href=\"https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/DTWEXBGS\">The broad dollar index rose from 123.5253 to 123.5978 on 20 Nov</a>, an older input consistent with pressure on buyers using other currencies. Gold still recovered. Reuters reported buying after last week’s drop, a flow the rates and currency data cannot measure.",
      "unsustainable": "Debt cannot keep growing faster than revenue indefinitely. Spending cuts, restructuring, transfers and monetary expansion shift the burden in different ways.",
      "would_change": "<a href=\"https://www.bea.gov/news/schedule/full\">October PCE and the second estimate of Q3 GDP arrive 25 Nov at 21:30 HKT</a>. If inflation cools while growth slows, expected tightening could ease. Persistent inflation could sustain positive real returns.",
